# The published contract is bytes, so its bytes must not depend on who checked it
# out. Without this, Git hands a Windows working tree CRLF copies of the schemas and
# the conformance corpus — and `npm pack` ships the working tree, so a release built
# there would send producers a different corpus from the one built anywhere else.
#
# Deliberately narrow. A repository-wide line-ending policy is a decision for the
# whole tree and everyone working in it; this is the surface that is *distributed*
# as an exact artifact, and it is the only one that needs settling here.
shared/schemas/*.json          text eol=lf
shared/conformance/**/*.json   text eol=lf
skills/structured-exchange/*   text eol=lf

# The Office and PDF fixtures are byte-exact documents, not text. Git guesses this
# from a NUL in the first few kilobytes and guesses wrong on an uncompressed PDF: it
# hands a Windows working tree a CRLF copy of `pdf-text.pdf`, 46 bytes longer than
# the blob, every xref offset past the first now wrong. pdf.js survives it by
# reindexing the whole document — which is what the "Indexing all PDF objects"
# warnings in the test output have been announcing — but a fixture carrying a
# deflated image stream would not survive at all, and would fail as an image that
# mysteriously will not decode. Declaring the type settles it for every fixture
# rather than leaving it to what bytes each one happens to contain.
server/test/fixtures/*.pdf     binary
server/test/fixtures/*.docx    binary
server/test/fixtures/*.dotx    binary
server/test/fixtures/*.pptx    binary
server/test/fixtures/*.potx    binary
server/test/fixtures/*.xlsx    binary
server/test/fixtures/*.png     binary
# A `.msg` is a compound file: every stream in it is addressed by a sector offset, so
# a CRLF conversion moves every offset after the first and the file stops being one.
server/test/fixtures/*.msg     binary

# A `.eml` is the opposite case, and is left to git on purpose: a message *is* text,
# and a Windows checkout handing the reader CRLF where a Linux one hands it LF is
# exactly the difference the reader has to tolerate. Declaring it would hide that.
